# Minimal-rule integration test for firewall-intent feature.
#
# Baseline (upstream today): ~13 rules required for the same intent
# (see fw-prior-state-tls-sni-allow on feat/fw-prior-state-fixtures for the
# 12-rule Prior_State expansion + 1 Decision_Hook rule that the auto-accept
# POC produces from a single author-visible line).
#
# Post-feature: 2 rules total, using tcp.session:, multi-value
# app-layer-protocol:, and the walker < operator.
#
# Rule 1 collapses the transport-layer session-lifecycle allow (was two
# separate flow:not_established + flow:established rules per the walker
# reference fixture ruletype-firewall-89-lt-sni) into a single hook rule
# that also latches to unknown|tls at the app-layer boundary, letting the
# pipeline flow through both the Detection_Window (unknown) and the TLS
# handshake states.
#
# Rule 2 uses the walker < operator to auto-accept every TLS state up to
# and including `client_hello_done`, where the author's SNI condition is
# evaluated — replacing the 7 TLS prerequisite rules that the Prior_State
# expansion would otherwise emit.

# Rule 1 (transport-layer allow): TCP session lifecycle + TLS/unknown app-layer
accept:hook tcp:all $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"TCP+TLS transport allow"; tcp.session:setup,established; app-layer-protocol:unknown|tls; sid:1000001;)

# Rule 2 (app-layer allow-by-SNI): walker < operator with tls.sni condition
accept:flow tls:<client_hello_done $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"TLS SNI allow www.google.com"; tls.sni; content:"www.google.com"; nocase; sid:1000002;)
